#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-3,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,0,5 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 1,2,3 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1)j+(-1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-10i+8j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-10,8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-10)+(-3)\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,2,-1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,-1,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\cdot (0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))+(-1)\\cdot (0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =20-2-2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $2\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times 3\\mathbf{c}) = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times 3c) & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times 3c)] & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =5 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-\\frac{5}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a97dd138-70f2-5d39-8ba1-22919ce2fa27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304214} {:answer/id #uuid "93887c8c-3dd7-5829-88d6-832b484164cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 1